From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 21:31:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 138/342] meson_vdec: HEVC, P and B pictures, 10 bits, the
 interrupt

The HEVC block now decodes Main and Main 10 streams (4:2:0, up to
1920x1088) to 8-bit NV12. The firmware only parses, so the host does the
rest, following ITU-T H.265: the picture order count (8.3.1), which
pictures stay references (8.3.2, the RPS), the reference lists with their
modifications (8.3.4), the co-located picture, RASL pictures skipped after
a fresh IRAP, and the output order by bumping (C.5). Frames are the
stream's reordering depth + 7, at most 16, each one area; they are kept
for the next session if the stream's are the same (a seek).

10-bit streams keep their references in Amlogic's compressed format, and
the frames handed out are the firmware's 8-bit "double write" (rounded);
meson_vdec_frame.offset says where the picture starts in the area.

A thread of the session answers the firmware, woken by the mailbox
interrupt (and every few milliseconds). At a stop it goes first, and the
firmware is let finish the slice it decodes (up to 200 ms, or 20 ms
without progress, without the rest of its stream): stopped and reset in
the middle of a picture, the block now and then hung the whole system.
Nothing is logged per session; a busy open says who has the decoder.

On the ODROID-C2, 8-bit streams with B-pyramids, 4 references, 4 slices a
picture and one starting at a CRA picture, and a 10-bit one, match FFmpeg
bit for bit (the 10-bit one rounded to 8 bits); 1080p decodes at about
277 frames/s.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
